  "worked_solution": "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-100 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 10\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}\u206110x+Q\\operatorname{sin}\u206110x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=10$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=50,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-10P\\operatorname{sin}\u206110x+10Q\\operatorname{cos}\u206110x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=50$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10P=50\\,\u27f9\\,P=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-5\\operatorname{cos}\u206110x+10\\operatorname{sin}\u206110x.\n$$",
